import meta::pure::metamodel::relation::*;
import meta::pure::functions::relation::*;

Enum meta::pure::functions::relation::JoinKind
{
  LEFT,
  RIGHT,
  FULL,
  INNER
}

'''
Navigates from a relation class mapping's source rows to the matching rows of `target`, keeping the
target's columns.

Only valid inside a Relation class mapping property, and only lowered to SQL: it cannot be executed
in memory. A source row matching several target rows yields one value per match.

A `.COLUMN` projection on the result only resolves when this call is the property's own value
expression (or one property access above it, e.g. `$src->navigationJoin(...).LEGALNAME`) -- calling
`navigationJoin` from inside a separate helper function and projecting a column there does not work.

**Parameters**
- `rel` — the source relation: `$src`, or another `navigationJoin`/`filterJoin`.
- `target` — a call to a relation function with no parameters, e.g. `my::firmRel()`.
- `cond` — the join condition, applied to a source row and a target row. Write it over its own
  parameters: `$row` (the mapped row) is not available in it.

**Returns** the matching target rows.

**Examples**
```pure
+firmName: String[1]: $src->navigationJoin(my::firmRel(), {p, f | $p.FIRMID == $f.ID}).LEGALNAME
```

**See also** `filterJoin(Relation<L>[1], Relation<R>[1], Function<{L[1], R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<L>[1]`
'''
function <<functionType.NotImplementedFunction>> meta::pure::functions::relation::navigationJoin<L,R>(rel:Relation<L>[1], target:Relation<R>[1], cond:Function<{L[1],R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<R>[1]
{
   fail('navigationJoin is a mapping-only, SQL-pushdown-only operator and cannot be executed in memory. It is only valid inside a Relation-mapping property expression.');
   $target;
}

'''
Same as `navigationJoin(Relation<L>[1], Relation<R>[1], Function<{L[1], R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<R>[1]`,
with an explicit join kind instead of the default `JoinKind.LEFT`. Neither kind excludes the owning
instance: a source row with no match keeps its row, empty on this property, under `LEFT` or `INNER`
alike -- `JoinKind.INNER` changes the generated SQL but not the result. `JoinKind.RIGHT`/`JoinKind.FULL`
are not supported.

**Examples**
```pure
+firmName: String[1]: $src->navigationJoin(my::firmRel(), JoinKind.INNER, {p, f | $p.FIRMID == $f.ID}).LEGALNAME
```
'''
function <<functionType.NotImplementedFunction>> meta::pure::functions::relation::navigationJoin<L,R>(rel:Relation<L>[1], target:Relation<R>[1], kind:JoinKind[1], cond:Function<{L[1],R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<R>[1]
{
   fail('navigationJoin is a mapping-only, SQL-pushdown-only operator and cannot be executed in memory. It is only valid inside a Relation-mapping property expression.');
   $target;
}

'''
Keeps a relation class mapping's source rows that match a row of `target`, with the source's columns.

Only valid inside a Relation class mapping property, and only lowered to SQL: it cannot be executed
in memory. This is not a semi-join: a source row matching several target rows is repeated.

A `.COLUMN` projection on the result only resolves when this call is the property's own value
expression (or one property access above it, e.g. `$src->filterJoin(...).NAME`) -- calling
`filterJoin` from inside a separate helper function and projecting a column there does not work.

**Parameters**
- `rel` — the source relation: `$src`, or another `navigationJoin`/`filterJoin`.
- `target` — a call to a relation function with no parameters, e.g. `my::firmRel()`.
- `cond` — the join condition, applied to a source row and a target row. Write it over its own
  parameters: `$row` (the mapped row) is not available in it.

**Returns** the matching source rows.

**Examples**
```pure
+employedName: String[1]: $src->filterJoin(my::firmRel(), {p, f | $p.FIRMID == $f.ID}).NAME
```

**See also** `navigationJoin(Relation<L>[1], Relation<R>[1], Function<{L[1], R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<R>[1]`
'''
function <<functionType.NotImplementedFunction>> meta::pure::functions::relation::filterJoin<L,R>(rel:Relation<L>[1], target:Relation<R>[1], cond:Function<{L[1],R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<L>[1]
{
   fail('filterJoin is a mapping-only, SQL-pushdown-only operator and cannot be executed in memory. It is only valid inside a Relation-mapping property expression.');
   $rel;
}

'''
Same as `filterJoin(Relation<L>[1], Relation<R>[1], Function<{L[1], R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<L>[1]`,
with an explicit join kind instead of the default `JoinKind.INNER` (a source row with no match keeps
its row, empty on this property; the owning instance itself is never excluded). `JoinKind.LEFT` turns
the gate off entirely: every source row gets this property from its own columns regardless of a match,
repeated once per matching target row if there is one. `JoinKind.RIGHT`/`JoinKind.FULL` are not
supported.

**Examples**
```pure
+employedName: String[1]: $src->filterJoin(my::firmRel(), JoinKind.LEFT, {p, f | $p.FIRMID == $f.ID}).NAME
```
'''
function <<functionType.NotImplementedFunction>> meta::pure::functions::relation::filterJoin<L,R>(rel:Relation<L>[1], target:Relation<R>[1], kind:JoinKind[1], cond:Function<{L[1],R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<L>[1]
{
   fail('filterJoin is a mapping-only, SQL-pushdown-only operator and cannot be executed in memory. It is only valid inside a Relation-mapping property expression.');
   $rel;
}

'''
Maps a Relation class mapping property, typed to a mapped Class, through a one-sided model join: a join
condition between an instance of the owning class and an instance of the property's class, written over
their properties -- exactly like the join condition of an association ModelJoin, e.g.
`{person, firm | $person.firmId == $firm.id}`.

The parameters are positional -- the first is the owning class, the second the property's class -- and
may optionally declare their types like an association ModelJoin's, e.g.
`{person: my::Person[1], firm: my::Firm[1] | ...}`. Local mapping properties (`+firmId: Integer[1]: ...`)
of either class mapping are visible to the condition. The target class mapping is the single root class
mapping (of any store) of the property's class, in the owning mapping or its includes.

Only valid as the entire value of a Relation class mapping property; the mapping compiler recognizes it
structurally, so this declaration is never matched and the call cannot be executed in memory.

**Parameters**
- `cond` — the join condition, applied to an instance of the owning class and one of the property's class.

**Returns** the associated instance of the property's class.

**Examples**
```pure
// parameter classes inferred: the owning class, then the property's class
+firm: my::Firm[1]: modelJoin({person, firm | $person.firmId == $firm.id})
// or declared explicitly, as in an association ModelJoin
+firm: my::Firm[1]: modelJoin({person: my::Person[1], firm: my::Firm[1] | $person.firmId == $firm.id})
```

**See also** `navigationJoin(Relation<L>[1], Relation<R>[1], Function<{L[1], R[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Relation<R>[1]`
'''
function meta::pure::functions::relation::modelJoin<S,T>(cond:Function<{S[1],T[1]->Boolean[1]}>[1]):Any[1]
{
   fail('modelJoin is a mapping-only operator resolved at mapping-compile-time and cannot be executed in memory. It is only valid as the entire value of a Relation-mapping property typed to a mapped Class.');
   1;
}
